# Empty on purpose: in production the API is reached through this same origin,
# proxied by the rewrites in vercel.json. api/client.ts then builds relative
# URLs like /api/auth/me/, so the session cookie is first-party and survives
# incognito's third-party cookie blocking. Served straight from onrender.com it
# is a third-party cookie, and Chrome blocks those in incognito by default --
# login succeeds, the cookie is dropped, and the next request arrives anonymous.
#
# vercel.json is where the proxy lives. It cannot hold comments (JSON, and
# Vercel's schema rejects unknown keys), so the two things worth knowing about
# it are here instead:
#
#   1. The /api rule must come FIRST. The rule below it is the SPA catch-all --
#      React Router owns the path, so an unknown URL has to serve index.html
#      rather than 404 -- and it would happily swallow /api too.
#   2. Django sets the session cookie with no Domain attribute, so the browser
#      scopes it to whatever host it asked. Through the rewrite that host is
#      the Vercel domain, which is the whole point.
#
# NOTE: a VITE_API_URL set in the Vercel dashboard takes precedence over this
# file. It has to be removed there, or the build will still point at
# onrender.com and the cookie will be third-party again.
VITE_API_URL=
